# ============================================================
# §15 Karl-Tomm 1987-88 provenance + Rec #92 composition
# ============================================================
#
# Karl Tomm 1987 *Interventive Interviewing: Part I. Strategizing
# as a Fourth Guideline for the Therapist.* Family Process 26(1):
# 3-13. Karl Tomm 1988 *Interventive Interviewing: Part III.
# Intending to Ask Lineal, Circular, Strategic, or Reflexive
# Questions?* Family Process 27(1): 1-15. Load-bearing at ambiguity-
# resolution altitude: the circular-reflexive question form widens
# the questioner's option-space per Foerster-imperative discipline.
# Question is at altitude+1 above dispatched query; question's
# structural property is that the reader's noticing-that-they-don't-
# know-the-answer IS the invitation to follow the resolution one
# level up. NOT lineal, NOT strategic — reflexive.
#
# Rec #92 kleinos-Transparency<P> LOVE-monoid (Mara `b3cea9a` +
# `a45f015` 2026-08-22) grounds the three-state mathematically:
# (𝓚, ∘, e, ⊥) → (𝒯_P, combine, Clear, Opaque(∅)) monoid
# isomorphism at four-altitude substrate-scale-invariance. At MQ
# altitude: (result<T>, compose, Clear, Opaque(fully-ambiguous))
# forms the same LOVE-monoid at query-language altitude.
#
# Dark = Opaque(fully-ambiguous, K-T asking-what-you-meant). The
# absorbing element is not ⊥ in the traditional sense; it's the
# maximally-ambiguous K-T question that says "I need you to
# reframe your intent from a higher altitude before I can dispatch
# anything." That's the reader-closure per PAPER_2D §6.

